        "problem": "$(5-x)^{3}=$",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(5-x)^{3},$ we have  $a=5,$ $b=-x,$ $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(5-x)^{3}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(5-x)}^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0}){(5)}^{3}{(-x)}^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1}){(5)}^{3-1}{(-x)}^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2}){(5)}^{3-2}{(-x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1{(5)}^{3}+3{(5)}^{2}{(-x)}^{1}+3{(5)}^{1}{(-x)}^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =125-75x+15{x}^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
